note arrpart mutant: `part A o k` keeps its operands and bounds checks, but evaluates k before o.
note The source expressions are still parsed in their original order. partord emits branch trampolines
note rather than moving instruction spans, so trap sites, deferred calls, inline fixups, global addresses
note and diagnostic source records retain their original emitted positions without relocation changes.
fn arrpart f ulong pi ulong md ulong to void
p = add mul sub f 1 6 pi
e = arrname
i = sub e 1
k = ulong get nm_kind i
if ge k 6
fail 7
end
if eq k 4
fail 19
end
fz_arg e
if eq get fn_pk p 3
if eq md 57
fail 20
end
if and ne k 1 ne k 3
fail 20
end
else
if eq md 58
fail 7
end
end
if ne ulong get nm_type i ulong get fn_pt p
fail 7
end
if ne get fn_pl p 0
fail 7
end
rn = alloc
ro = partord rn
d = partck i ro rn
ux = add mul get depth 0 6 pi
put ul_k ux 1
put ul_v ux rn
put rt 0 e
put rk 0 5
put rv 0 d
end

note partord: emitted layout is B length; offset; B checks; length; B offset; checks.
note Reserve rn before lowering offset, so offset temporaries cannot overwrite it and offset calls save
note and restore it through the existing register-save machinery. No new runtime stack convention is used.
note Force the length result into rn before jumping back to offset; ro remains the offset result.
fn partord rn ulong to ulong
first = get oc 0
emit 335544320
head = get oc 0
rdval 0 5 255 0
if ne get rt 0 5
fail 7
end
ro = matreg get rk 0 get rv 0 5
done = get oc 0
emit 335544320
patch26 first get oc 0
rdval 0 5 255 0
if ne get rt 0 5
fail 7
end
force get rk 0 get rv 0 5 rn
back head
patch26 done get oc 0
ret ro
end
